 "redistricting_notes": "Mid-decade redraws reshaped ~8 states. Texas: legislature drew 5 new R-leaning seats (incl. new TX-9, TX-32, TX-35); a federal panel enjoined the map Nov 18, 2025 but SCOTUS stayed and then allowed it 6-3 on Dec 4, 2025. California: Prop 50 passed with ~64% (Nov 2025), redrawing to target 5 GOP seats (CA-1, CA-3, CA-22, CA-41/CA-48 area, CA-6 now D+5; new CA-38). Florida: special session map signed May 4, 2026 targeting 3-5 R gains (new FL-22; FL-9 Soto and FL-14 Castor now in R-leaning seats). North Carolina: Oct 2025 redraw made NC-1 (Don Davis) R+5. Ohio: unanimous compromise map (Oct 2025) made OH-1 more competitive, OH-9 more R, OH-13 more D. Louisiana: after Louisiana v. Callais (Apr 2026) a map signed May 29 eliminated one of two Black-plurality D seats (LA-6). Alabama: new map removes AL-2's D lean (now R+7); state court block stayed by SCOTUS Jun 2, 2026. Tennessee: TN-9 (Memphis) split, now Safe R. Utah: court-ordered map creates a safe-D Salt Lake seat (UT-1). Virginia: Democrats' redistricting amendment invalidated May 8, 2026 (old lines). Missouri: 2025 map targeting MO-5 blocked by referendum; 2022 map in place. Maryland, Indiana, Kansas efforts failed; Wisconsin challenge dismissed Mar 31, 2026; SCOTUS blocked an NY-11 redraw. Net on-paper effect: roughly R +6 to +9 (TX, FL, NC, OH, AL, LA, TN) offset by D +5 to +6 (CA, UT), i.e. about R +1 to +4 net, but forecasters (DDHQ, Silver) conclude Republicans gain little or nothing net because the strong D environment flips several of the new marginal R seats; the main effect is fewer true swing seats (Silver: D gain ~22 seats on average despite a ~D+9 environment).",
